<div class="docs-generation-page">

    <div class="docs-eyebrow">ENGINE / ASYNC WORK</div>

    <h1>
        World generation <span class="docs-accent">&amp; jobs</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Chunk streaming, generation, lighting initialization, and mesh
        building are staged through background jobs so the main update
        loop does not perform every operation synchronously.
    </p>

    <h2>Streaming around the player</h2>

    <p>
        <code>ChunkStreamingSystem</code> observes the player's chunk
        coordinate. When the player crosses a chunk boundary, it builds
        a required set inside a circular radius based on
        <code>WorldConfiguration.StreamDistance</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Missing chunks are sorted by squared distance so nearer chunks
        are queued first. The system caps concurrent loads and mesh
        builds through <code>MaxConcurrentLoads</code> and
        <code>MaxConcurrentMeshes</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Chunk load sequence</h2>

    <ol class="docs-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Queue a load</strong>
            <p>
                The streaming system submits a <code>LoadChunkJob</code>
                for a required coordinate.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Load or generate</strong>
            <p>
                The job checks for an existing chunk; otherwise
                <code>ChunkLoader.Load</code> loads the saved chunk or
                generates one and registers it with the world.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Initialize lighting</strong>
            <p>
                The load job initializes sky light for the chunk.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Publish completion</strong>
            <p>
                The job puts the completed chunk into a concurrent
                queue, which the streaming system drains on its update.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Build mesh</strong>
            <p>
                The system queues a <code>BuildChunkMeshJob</code>
                and tracks it with a <code>JobFence</code>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Mesh building</h2>

    <p>
        <code>BuildChunkMeshJob</code> iterates all 26 vertical section
        layers and the 2 × 2 horizontal section positions. It calls
        <code>MeshBuilder.BuildSectionMesh</code> and publishes the
        built sections through <code>WorldMeshManager</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Empty opaque vertex output is skipped by the current job
        implementation.
    </p>

    <h2>Block mutation and dirty mesh updates</h2>

    <p>
        Breaking or placing a block calls <code>World.SetBlock</code>.
        The <code>BlockChanged</code> event updates lighting and marks
        mesh data dirty.
    </p>

    <p>
        <code>WorldMeshUpdateSystem</code> sees dirty blocks and submits
        a <code>WorldUpdateJob</code> to rebuild affected sections.
    </p>

    <h2>Job system model</h2>

    <p>
        The repository's architecture notes describe a worker pool sized
        to <code>max(2, processor count - 1)</code>, consuming a priority
        queue.
    </p>

    <p>
        Jobs are submitted with a priority and optionally a
        <code>JobFence</code>. A fence tracks completion so a system
        can poll <code>IsComplete</code> or wait for the group of jobs.
    </p>

    <h2>Current terrain generator</h2>

    <p>
        The checked-in <code>ChunkGenerator</code> is a simple flat
        generator:
    </p>

